  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCurrent analysis reveals that the primary axis of the 500mb trough \nis just to our east over the Central Plains. At the surface, the \ncold front has cleared our area bringing much cooler temperatures \nand clear skies. A surface high continues to build in over the \nCentral Plains, pushing up against the retreating cold front, \nresulting in a fairly tight pressure gradient over the forecast \narea. This will help keep breezy conditions around through this \nevening with NNW winds around 15-20 mph and gusts up to 30 mph. As \nthe 500mb trough and surface cold front exit our region, the surface \nhigh will continue to build in over the Northern Plains and Upper \nMidwest resulting in light northerly flow and clear skies overnight \nand Monday. Clear skies overnight will allow for radiational cooling \nthat drops lows into the 20s. Another upper level trough will move \nin over the Central Plains Monday night into Tuesday with a \nsaturated profile above 450mb. This will result in a high stratus \ndeck Tuesday that will keep afternoon highs in the upper 30s and low \n40s. Meanwhile, at the surface, high pressure will be centered over \nIowa resulting in light NE flow over the forecast area. The upper-\nlevel trough will then deepen over the Midwest Wednesday and keep \nsome lingering high clouds around, especially for northern MO, which \nwill keep highs in the low to mid 40s. By Thursday, the upper-level \ntrough will take on a negative tilt but model guidance keeps the \nprimary axis of this trough east of our forecast area so \nThanksgiving Day should be dry and mostly sunny with highs in the \nmid to upper 40s. A warming trend begins Friday as the upper-level \ntrough moves off to the east, allowing quasi-zonal flow to settle \nover MO. Meanwhile, a surface low moving across the US/Canadian \nborder will support more westerly flow and WAA into the area, and \nensemble guidance shows a 60-90% chance of highs above 50 by \nSaturday.",
